  "analysis": "Comparing candidate platforms against selection criteria reveals a layered tradeoff between orchestration maturity, typed-output guarantees, and routing flexibility. DeepAgents (source_001) sits atop LangGraph (source_002), inheriting graph state machine semantics that suit complex multi-step workflows, but this coupling means adopting DeepAgents locks the platform into LangChain's orchestration model and its abstraction overhead. Pydantic AI (source_003) offers strong typed tool definitions and structured outputs, which improves reliability of per-node scoring and validation, yet it lacks native graph workflow primitives, so a router built on it would need external orchestration. The routing research gap (source_005) frames the core selection criterion: per-node model selection must move the quality-cost-latency Pareto frontier, which neither DeepAgents nor Pydantic AI addresses natively; both are application-layer frameworks, not routers. The task decomposition boundary (source_006) clarifies that static DAG construction belongs to the application layer, so the platform comparison should weight how well each candidate consumes a fixed DAG and scores per-node outcomes rather than how it decomposes tasks. DSH (source_004) is explicitly reserved for the outer validation environment, not the core routing loop, so it should be excluded from the inner-loop comparison and treated as a validation harness instead. The benchmark design (source_007) sets the measurable criteria: fixed task DAGs, multiple candidate models, per-node and per-task scoring, cost accounting, and critical-path latency. Against these, DeepAgents scores well on workflow orchestration and multi-model integration via LangGraph, but its harness abstraction may obscure per-node cost and latency attribution. Pydantic AI scores well on structured outputs that make scoring deterministic, but it provides no built-in cost accounting or latency instrumentation. The tradeoff is therefore: orchest